{
  "question_id": "Plane397",
  "variant_id": "r56_square_object_unit_across_scales",
  "stage": "material_refinement",
  "created_at": "2026-11-01T20:42:27.861634+00:00",
  "expected_wrong_answer": "A",
  "correct_answer": "D",
  "mechanism": "Use one open square-corner frame on EVERY existing square object: nine unit cells and three3-by3 squares. cell00 supplies a small square object as the reference; the three much larger squares receive the same local square-corner role. Target treating each framed square as one equal count unit: seven small grey plus one large grey among twelve squares gives8/12=2/3 (A), instead of weighting large square area9 and obtaining4/9.",
  "old_new_difference": "Unit20 had no candidate or calls and rejected uniform grey-border emphasis without a genuine part match. New design frames all12 real square objects, including the four white ones, rather than uniformly emphasizing only grey. Cross-scale homologous bottom/right corners make the square-object unit reference/receiver explicit. Related Plane340 r34 used equal-size containing frames to replace PARTIAL grey regions with whole unit areas, preset2 and finals0.75/1: that is a different whole-frame-versus-part mechanism. Here every square is uniformly grey/white, and the error is COUNT weighting across1-to3 side scales. No prior PNG is copied or relabelled.",
  "primary_unique_math_proof": "Let the small square side be u. The subdivided quadrant contains a3-by3 array, hence its side3u. The other three unsubdivided squares share matching full quadrant sides and each have area9u^2. The whole outer square side6u, area36u^2. Seven of the nine unit squares and exactly one of the three larger squares are grey. Grey area=(7+9)u^2=16u^2, fraction16/36=4/9, uniquely D. Equal object counting would instead give(7+1)/(9+3)=8/12=2/3 (A). This is a square-count versus area-weight error: the actual side3-to1 distinction remains visible. The question asks a grey area fraction, not how many painted outlines or marks exist.",
